    66	## Harness abstraction
    67	
    68	`skills/sdd/SKILL.md` is the harness-neutral entry: process skeleton, Step-0 procedure,
    69	dispatch overrides, flavour choice, controller rules — written without harness-specific
    70	tool names. It begins with: "identify your harness; read `harnesses/<harness>.md` first."
    71	
    72	Each adapter defines the mapping for exactly five concerns:
    73	
    74	| Concern | claude-code.md | codex.md |
    75	| --- | --- | --- |
    76	| Load superpowers SDD skill | Skill tool (`superpowers:subagent-driven-development`) | native skill loading per superpowers' codex adaptation |
    77	| Native subagent dispatch | Agent tool | `spawn_agent` |
    78	| Task tracking | TodoWrite | `update_plan` |
    79	| Background jobs & notifications | Bash `run_in_background` + task notifications | shell job + polling pattern |
    80	| Asset root resolution | `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` | repo-relative from the physical SKILL.md |
    81	
    82	**Path rule:** everywhere outside `harnesses/claude-code.md`, asset references are
    83	relative to the plugin tree root, resolved from the physical location of the selected
    84	SKILL.md (`<root>/skills/sdd/SKILL.md` → `<root>/…`). `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` never
    85	appears in core/, providers/, contracts/, or the neutral SKILL.md.
    86	
    87	**Lever rename:** "all Claude" becomes **`native-subagents`** (dispatch via the
    88	controller harness's own subagent mechanism; superpowers stock behavior). "all Claude"
    89	remains documented in the claude-code adapter as an alias.
    90	
    91	**Packaging:** one canonical tree serves both harnesses. Claude Code installs via
    92	`.claude-plugin/` marketplace as today. Codex installs by cloning the whole repository
    93	(the skill is useless without core/ and providers/ siblings); `codex/INSTALL.md` gives
    94	the CONCRETE discovery steps per superpowers' own codex adaptation
    95	(`superpowers/skills/using-superpowers/references/codex-tools.md`): where to register the
    96	skill so codex loads it, and how `<root>` is derived (three dirname steps up from the
    97	physical `skills/sdd/SKILL.md`). A `scripts/codex-smoke` check (part of validate-packs
    98	`--step0` or standalone) verifies from a fresh checkout: SKILL.md at the expected
    99	relative path, `harnesses/codex.md` present, root derivation resolves core/ and
   100	providers/, validator passes — runnable WITHOUT codex installed; full codex-driven
   101	end-to-end remains backlog. Version lives in `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and is
   102	mirrored in the README header; a validator check keeps them in sync.
   103	
   104	## The pack contract
   105	
   106	A directory under `providers/` is a valid pack iff it contains `pack.md`, `models.md`,
   107	`verification-log.md`, and `pack.md` starts with a **declarative** front-matter manifest
   108	— data only, no executable shell strings:
   109	
   110	```yaml
   111	---
   112	schema-version: 1
   113	id: opencode                    # MUST equal the directory name; [a-z0-9-]+
   114	cli: opencode                   # executable NAME only ([a-z0-9-]+); detection = PATH lookup of this name
   115	verified-version: "1.17.18"     # version the pack's facts were verified against
   116	version-argv: ["opencode", "--version"]
   117	resume-argv: ["opencode", "run", "-s", "{session_id}"]   # {session_id} is the only placeholder
   118	fork-flag: "--fork"             # optional
   119	session-source: session-list    # enum: session-list | exec-output | conversation-id
   120	session-list-argv: ["opencode", "session", "list"]       # required iff session-source: session-list
   121	stall-signal: log-age           # enum: log-age | process+print-timeout
   122	sandbox: none                   # enum: enforced | none
   123	readiness-argv: ["opencode", "session", "list"]   # optional; default = version-argv
   124	readiness-timeout-seconds: 30   # optional; default 30
   125	---
   126	```
   127	
   128	**Front-matter grammar (not general YAML):** a restricted `key: value` grammar — keys
   129	`[a-z-]+`; values are unquoted/double-quoted scalars or JSON arrays of strings on one
   130	line. This is exactly what the stdlib validator parses; anything else is a validity error.
   131	
   132	**Executable-identity rule:** for EVERY `*-argv` field, `argv[0]` MUST equal `cli`.
   133	Remaining elements must be flags/subcommands/placeholders (no absolute paths, no shell
   134	metacharacters `;|&<>$` — validator-enforced). Combined with the PATH-lookup detection
   135	rule this means a pack can only ever cause the declared CLI binary to run.
   136	**Trust gate:** Step 0 refuses to use any pack tree that does not pass
   137	`scripts/validate-packs` (cheap, stdlib, runs in <1s) — a copied/modified pack is
   138	validated before anything in it is executed or followed.
   139	
   140	- **Detection executes nothing pack-authored**: Step 0 does a PATH lookup of the
   141	  validated `cli` name (`command -v -- "<cli>"` after the validator confirms the name
   142	  matches `[a-z0-9-]+`). Argv fields are executed only after full schema validation
   143	  (including the argv[0]==cli rule), only for providers that pass detection, with the
   144	  closed placeholder set, under `readiness-timeout-seconds`.
   145	- The body of `pack.md` is prose (version-stamped): canonical dispatch template, verified
   146	  behavior, gotchas, auth notes, output conventions. The pack's template is the ONLY
   147	  dispatch template for that CLI — the skill carries the abstract dispatch shape, not
   148	  per-CLI incantations.
   149	
   150	### `models.md`
   151	
   152	| Tier | Lane | Priority | Model id | Status | Pricing | Rationale |
   153	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   154	
   155	- **Tier** ∈ `cheapest | standard | most-capable` (from core/roles.md).
   156	- **Lane** ∈ `implement | review | any`.
   157	- **Status** closed enum: `verified | experimental | unavailable | superseded | rejected`.
   158	  **Eligible for resolution: `verified`, `experimental` only.**
   159	- Documentary content — rejected models, watch list, superseded history — lives in
   160	  separate sections BELOW the resolvable table (rejected/watch rows there carry evidence
   161	  links into the pack's verification-log; they are outside resolution by construction).
   162	- Validity: within each (tier, lane) pair, priorities are unique positive integers and
   163	  exactly one row has priority 1. Row order carries no meaning.
   164	
   165	### Resolution algorithm (exact)
   166	
   167	Input: role, provider, exclusions (a possibly-empty set of (provider, model) pairs from
   168	this session's ledger). Steps:
   169	1. role → (tier, lane) via core/roles.md (lane is `implement` for implementer roles,
   170	   `review` for reviewer roles).
   171	2. In the provider's resolvable table, filter to Status ∈ {verified, experimental} and
   172	   drop excluded (provider, model) pairs.
   173	3. **Candidate order** = rows matching (tier, lane) exactly, ascending priority, followed
   174	   by rows matching (tier, `any`), ascending priority.
   175	4. Resolve to the FIRST candidate. Fallback (below) means advancing to the next candidate
   176	   in this same order — the order is the complete fallback sequence.
   177	5. If no candidate exists → explicit resolution error, surfaced to the user. Never
   178	   substitute across tiers automatically; never substitute across providers automatically
   179	   (see Failure classes — cross-provider moves are always a user decision).
   180	
   181	### Routing precedence (which provider)
   182	
   183	**Step 0 of routing — before provider selection:** if the `native-subagents` lever (or a
   184	per-task native directive) is in effect, external dispatch is bypassed entirely — the
   185	harness's native subagent mechanism (per the adapter) handles the task and NO provider is
   186	selected. `native-subagents` is a dispatch mode, not a provider, and is never an input to
   187	resolution.
   188	
   189	Otherwise, the provider for a dispatch is chosen by the first match:
   190	1. Explicit per-task provider directive in the plan.
   191	2. Session routing lever ("via agy", "delegate mechanical to opencode").
   192	3. Config `providers_by_lane` (per-lane provider map), then config `default_provider`.
   193	4. Built-in default: `codex` if active (structured-review contract, sandbox), else the
   194	   only active provider **iff exactly one is active**.
   195	5. Otherwise: stop with a route-selection question to the user.
   196	
   197	Naming an INACTIVE provider at any step → surface to the user (reroute or abort); never
   198	silently reroute.
   199	
   200	### Failure classes & fallback state
   201	
   202	- **Channel/capability failures** (auth failure, model-not-found, startup stall,
   203	  repeated zero-output hang): automatic fallback IS allowed — advance to the next
   204	  candidate in the resolution order (same provider; the order already encodes exact-lane
   205	  then `any`). Maximum **3 total dispatch attempts** per (task, role); exhausting them, or
   206	  any need to change PROVIDER, is a user question — cross-provider rerouting is never
   207	  automatic, consistent with the routing rule.
   208	- **Ledger record** (one line per attempt, in the SDD progress ledger):
   209	  `model-attempt: task=<N> role=<role> provider=<id> model=<id> class=<channel|quality> outcome=<failed|ok>`
   210	  Exclusion scope: a `channel`-failed (provider, model) pair is excluded session-wide
   211	  (channel failures are provider/model-level, not task-level); `quality` failures create
   212	  NO exclusion — they route to escalation. Post-compaction sessions rebuild exclusions
   213	  from these ledger lines.
   214	- **Quality failures** (implementer BLOCKED, reviewer rejects repeatedly, gate failures):
   215	  NEVER any automatic fallback — escalate tier or adjudicate with the user, per the
   216	  superpowers status-handling rules.
   217	
   218	## Provider states & readiness
   219	
   220	`command -v` proves installation, not usability. Step 0 models four states:
   221	- **installed** — PATH lookup of `cli` succeeds.
   222	- **compatible** — run `version-argv` (bounded by `readiness-timeout-seconds`); extract
   223	  the first `[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)+` match from its output; compatible iff it string-equals
   224	  `verified-version`. Mismatch, command failure, or no parseable version → WARN (pack
   225	  facts may be stale; point to `sdd-dispatch-verify <id>`). With config
   226	  `require-verified-version: true`, a provider that is not compatible is removed from the
   227	  ACTIVE set (hard block), and Step 0 says so.
   228	- **ready** — run `readiness-argv` (default: `version-argv`) with the timeout; ready iff
   229	  exit 0. Checked lazily, ONLY for the provider actually selected, before its first
   230	  dispatch. Not-ready → channel-class failure (user question or documented fallback —
   231	  never silent). A readiness probe proves the CLI answers; it does not guarantee model
   232	  access — model-not-found at dispatch remains a channel failure.
   233	- **active** — installed ∧ not disabled by config ∧ (compatible if required by config).
   234	
   235	## Configuration (replaces providers.local.json)
   236	
   237	Machine/project policy lives OUTSIDE the plugin tree (plugin caches are replaced on
   238	upgrade). Search order, first found wins:
   239	1. `$SDD_DISPATCH_CONFIG` (explicit path)
   240	2. `<project>/.sdd-dispatch.json` (project policy, committable)
   241	3. `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/sdd-dispatch/config.json` (user/machine policy)
   242	
   243	Schema (all keys optional):
   244	```json
   245	{
   246	  "disable": ["codex"],
   247	  "default_provider": "opencode",
   248	  "providers_by_lane": { "implement": "opencode", "review": "codex" },
   249	  "require-verified-version": false,
   250	  "note": "free text"
   251	}
   252	```
   253	Types (closed): `disable` array of pack ids; `default_provider` pack id;
   254	`providers_by_lane` object with keys ⊆ {implement, review} and pack-id values;
   255	`require-verified-version` boolean; `note` string.
   256	Rules: config can only disable/steer — never enables an undetected CLI. Fail closed
   257	(specific, actionable error; never silently proceed with defaults) on: malformed JSON;
   258	wrong types; unknown provider ids anywhere; `default_provider` or any
   259	`providers_by_lane` value naming a disabled provider; `$SDD_DISPATCH_CONFIG` set but
   260	missing/unreadable. Duplicates in `disable` are ignored. Unknown top-level keys → warn,
   261	ignore. Empty active set after disables → error naming the disables.
   262	
